WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.957
Welcome to Module 6, Joining Data in PostgreSQL.

00:00:03.957 --> 00:00:09.022
Up to this point, many of our examples have focused on one table at a time.

00:00:09.022 --> 00:00:17.542
That is useful for learning SQL, but real analytical work usually requires information that is stored across

00:00:17.542 --> 00:00:17.922
several related tables.

00:00:17.922 --> 00:00:24.187
A healthcare system may store patients in one table, admissions in another, lab results in another,

00:00:24.187 --> 00:00:26.857
and staff information somewhere else.

00:00:26.857 --> 00:00:32.597
A university may separate students, registrations, courses, and grades.

00:00:32.597 --> 00:00:38.017
An online store may separate customers, orders, products, and payments.

00:00:38.017 --> 00:00:43.307
In this module, the important skill is not simply memorizing JOIN syntax.

00:00:43.307 --> 00:00:46.687
I want you to think about what the final rows mean.

00:00:46.687 --> 00:00:53.102
If one patient has several admissions, should that patient appear once or several times?

00:00:53.102 --> 00:00:57.217
If a patient has no admission, should that patient disappear,

00:00:57.217 --> 00:01:00.787
or should the patient remain with missing admission values?

00:01:00.787 --> 00:01:04.097
Those questions determine how we join the data.

00:01:04.097 --> 00:01:09.257
As we work through the module, keep four questions in mind.

00:01:09.257 --> 00:01:12.887
First, what should one output row represent?

00:01:12.887 --> 00:01:16.837
Second, which rows must be preserved even when no match exists?

00:01:16.837 --> 00:01:21.477
Third, what rule tells us that two rows belong together?

00:01:21.477 --> 00:01:27.852
And fourth, after the join runs, how can we validate that the result makes sense?

00:01:27.852 --> 00:01:34.094
If you build the habit of answering those four questions, JOINs become much easier to reason about.
